    "intro": "Every shift in a SOC begins and ends at the same place: the SIEM. It is the single pane of glass where logs from firewalls, endpoints, identity providers, and cloud platforms converge, get normalized, and get evaluated against detection logic. When an alert lands in your queue, it did not appear by magic. Some log event was collected, parsed, normalized, stored, and then matched by a rule a detection engineer wrote. Understand that entire journey (from a raw log line to a red alert on your screen) and you will triage faster and stop treating alerts as verdicts handed down from an oracle.\n\nThis lesson walks you through the full life of an alert. First you will learn what a SIEM is and the ingestion pipeline that feeds it: collect, parse, normalize, store, search. Then you will see how detection rules turn stored events into alerts: signatures, thresholds, cross-source correlation, and scheduled analytics. From there we reach the heart of the analyst's craft: the triage decision. You will learn the precise definitions of true positive, false positive, and the tricky benign true positive, and why a missed true positive costs far more than a wasted hour on a false one.\n\nThen we get practical. You will learn the data model you are actually querying (indexes, sourcetypes and tables, event time versus arrival time), and real side-by-side KQL and SPL triage queries over Sentinel's SigninLogs and Splunk's sign-in data. You will learn to read the alert object itself (entities, custom details, the link back to raw events) and how to ask for a rule to be tuned without blinding it. Finally you will learn enrichment: turning a bare alert into a verdict with asset, user, and threat-intelligence context, and deciding when to close, keep digging, or escalate.\n\nBy the end you will ask the questions a good analyst asks: What rule fired this, and on what logic? Is the entity a high-value asset or a test box? Has this user done this before? What single query would confirm or kill my hypothesis fastest? Those questions, not memorized playbooks, separate an analyst who clears a queue from one who catches an intrusion.",
