        "content": "A SIEM (Security Information and Event Management platform) is best understood as the central nervous system of a security operation. Nerves carry signals from every part of the body to the brain, which correlates them and decides what to react to. A SIEM does the same with logs: it pulls signals from firewalls, endpoints, identity providers, servers, and cloud services into one place where they can be correlated and acted on. Splunk, Microsoft Sentinel, Elastic Security, IBM QRadar, and Google Security Operations (formerly Chronicle) are all SIEMs. They differ in query language and pricing model, but the pipeline underneath them is the same five stages.\n\n### Collect\n\nThe first stage is collection. **Log sources**, any system that produces logs, such as a firewall, a server, an identity provider, or a cloud application. Ship their events to the SIEM by forwarders (a Splunk Universal Forwarder on a host), by syslog (a firewall pushing UDP/514 to a collector), by API polling (Sentinel pulling Microsoft Entra ID, formerly Azure AD, sign-ins through the Microsoft Graph connector), or by agents (an Elastic Beat). At this stage the data is raw: a firewall's native key-value string, a Windows Event XML blob, a JSON document from a cloud API. Nothing is standardized yet. A gap here is the most common cause of a blind spot: if a critical server is not forwarding logs, no rule downstream can ever fire on it.\n\n### Parse and Normalize\n\nParsing breaks the raw event into fields. A Palo Alto traffic log becomes discrete values for source IP, destination IP, action, and bytes. Normalization then maps those vendor-specific field names onto a common schema so that a query written once works across many sources. The Elastic Common Schema (ECS) calls the source address source.ip; Splunk's Common Information Model (CIM) calls it src. Both solve the same problem: without normalization, you would need a different query for every vendor's idea of what to name an IP address. When you see fields like user.name, event.action, or DeviceName, you are looking at normalized data. Which parser runs depends on how the source shipped the event: plain syslog, CEF, LEEF, JSON from an API, or Windows XML. The newer Open Cybersecurity Schema Framework (OCSF) tries to move normalization upstream so that sources emit one shared JSON schema in the first place; Amazon Security Lake, for example, stores its data in OCSF.\n\n### Store and Search\n\nThe normalized events are indexed into storage optimized for time-series search. This is where retention policy lives. Hot storage for the last thirty or ninety days that queries fast, and cheaper cold or archive tiers for older data you may need during an investigation. Search is the stage you live in as an analyst: you write a query, the engine scans the relevant indexes over a time range, and returns matching events. Understanding that a query only sees what was collected, correctly parsed, and still within retention explains most of the frustrating moments in triage. If your search returns nothing, the first question is not always \"is the host clean\", sometimes it is \"is this source even being ingested, and does my time range cover it.\"",
        "codeExample": "// Conceptual view of the SIEM ingestion pipeline (not executable)\n// Stage 1 COLLECT   -> raw event arrives from the source\n//   Palo Alto syslog: \"...src=10.4.2.9 dst=52.1.8.3 action=allow bytes=48213...\"\n// Stage 2 PARSE     -> raw string split into fields\n//   src=10.4.2.9  dst=52.1.8.3  action=allow  bytes=48213\n// Stage 3 NORMALIZE -> vendor fields mapped to a common schema (ECS shown)\n//   source.ip=10.4.2.9  destination.ip=52.1.8.3  event.action=allow  network.bytes=48213\n// Stage 4 STORE     -> indexed into time-series storage with a retention tier\n//   index=firewall  _time=2026-07-04T09:14:22Z  (hot tier, 90-day retention)\n// Stage 5 SEARCH    -> analyst query scans the index over a time window\n//   index=firewall source.ip=10.4.2.9 earliest=-24h | table _time destination.ip action network.bytes",
