        "heading": "How an Alert Is Born: Detection Rules and Correlation",
        "content": "Stored events do nothing on their own. What creates an alert is a detection rule. Logic that runs against the indexed data and raises a signal when its conditions are met. Understanding the shape of these rules tells you what an alert can and cannot prove, which is exactly what you need for good triage. There are four broad families of detection logic, and most real detections are a blend.\n\n### Signatures\n\nThe simplest rule is a signature: a match on a known-bad indicator. A hash on a threat-intel blocklist, a specific process command line, a User-Agent string tied to a malware family, a domain from an IOC feed. Signatures are high precision and low recall, when they fire they are usually meaningful, but they only catch what someone has already seen and written down. An attacker who changes one byte of a payload changes its hash and slips past a hash signature entirely.\n\n### Thresholds\n\nA threshold rule counts events and fires when the count crosses a line within a time window. Ten failed logons for one account in five minutes. Fifty distinct destination hosts contacted on port 445 in ten minutes. Thresholds catch brute force, enumeration, and scanning. Behaviors defined by volume rather than by a single bad event. The art is in the number: too low and you drown in false positives, too high and a slow-and-low attacker walks under it. When you triage a threshold alert, always ask what the threshold was and whether the volume looks like an attack or like a batch job, a login storm after an outage, or a vulnerability scanner.\n\n### Correlation Across Sources\n\nThe highest-fidelity detections correlate events from different sources that individually look benign. A single failed VPN login is noise. A failed VPN login from a new country, followed by a successful login, followed by a mailbox forwarding-rule creation in Office 365, is a credential-compromise story, and no single source could tell it. Correlation rules join on a shared pivot, usually user.name or source.ip, and fire only when the combination appears. This is why identity, endpoint, and cloud logs all need to reach the same SIEM: correlation is only possible across data that lives together.\n\n### Scheduled Analytics\n\nModern SIEMs run analytics rules on a schedule (every five minutes, every hour) that execute a saved query and generate an alert (in Sentinel, an incident) for each result. This is how most KQL and SPL detections actually run in production. The rule has a query, a frequency, a threshold on result count, and entity mappings that tell the platform which fields are the user, the host, and the IP so the resulting alert is investigable. When you open an alert, find and read the rule query. It is the single most useful artifact for triage, because it tells you exactly what condition the platform believes it saw, and lets you judge whether that condition is actually suspicious in this context.\n\n| Detection Type | How It Works | What It Catches |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Signatures | Matches a known-bad indicator (hash, command line, User-Agent, domain) | Known threats already seen and written down; high precision, low recall |\n| Thresholds | Counts events and fires when the count crosses a line within a time window | Brute force, enumeration, and scanning. Behaviors defined by volume |\n| Correlation across sources | Joins events from different sources on a shared pivot such as user.name or source.ip | Multi-step stories no single source could tell; highest-fidelity |\n| Scheduled analytics | Runs a saved query on a schedule and generates an alert per result | How most KQL and SPL detections run in production |",
        "codeExample": "// KQL analytics rule (Microsoft Sentinel): password-spray detection on Microsoft Entra ID (Azure AD) sign-ins\n// Fires when one source IP fails logons against MANY distinct accounts in a short window,\n// which is the signature of spraying one common password across an org.\nSigninLogs\n| where TimeGenerated > ago(1h)\n| where ResultType != \"0\"                     // ResultType is a string: \"0\" = success, anything else = failure\n| summarize\n    FailedAttempts   = count(),\n    TargetedAccounts = dcount(UserPrincipalName),\n    Accounts         = make_set(UserPrincipalName, 20)\n    by IPAddress, bin(TimeGenerated, 15m)\n| where TargetedAccounts >= 10 and FailedAttempts >= 15\n| project TimeGenerated, IPAddress, TargetedAccounts, FailedAttempts, Accounts\n| sort by TargetedAccounts desc\n// Entity mapping in the rule: IPAddress -> IP entity, so the alert is pivotable."
