        "heading": "True Positive, False Positive, and the Triage Decision",
        "content": "Triage is the act of deciding what an alert really means and what to do about it. Every alert you touch resolves into one of a small set of dispositions, and knowing the definitions precisely keeps you from the two career-limiting mistakes: closing a real intrusion as noise, and burning hours chasing a phantom.\n\nTwo words are easy to blur and worth pinning down first. An **alert** is a single detection firing: one rule matched, one moment, one entity. An **incident** (some platforms call it a case) is the broader container an analyst, or the platform itself, builds by grouping one or more related alerts into the story of what actually happened: a single intrusion might raise separate alerts across identity, endpoint, and network that all belong to one incident. You triage individual alerts, but you often escalate into, or open, an incident once you can see they connect.\n\n### The Four Outcomes\n\nA **true positive** is an alert that correctly identifies genuinely malicious or unauthorized activity. The password spray was real; the attacker was really spraying. A **false positive** is an alert that fired but the underlying activity is not malicious: a vulnerability scanner tripping a port-scan rule, an admin script tripping a lateral-movement rule. A **true negative** is the silent, correct non-alert: nothing bad happened and nothing fired. You never see these, but a healthy detection generates them constantly. A **false negative** is the dangerous one: something bad happened and no alert fired at all. False negatives are invisible in your queue by definition, which is why threat hunting and detection engineering exist, to find what the rules are missing.\n\n### The Benign True Positive\n\nThere is a fourth disposition that trips up junior analysts: the **benign true positive**. Here the alert correctly identified the exact behavior it was written to catch (it is not a false positive, the rule worked perfectly) but the activity, while real, is authorized and harmless. A penetration tester running an approved engagement will trip dozens of your lateral-movement and credential-access rules.\n\nThose are all true positives in the technical sense: the tooling really did what the rule detected. But the disposition is benign because the activity was sanctioned. The right close-out is not \"false positive\" (which implies the rule is broken and invites someone to tune it down) but \"benign true positive. Authorized pentest, ticket CHG-4471.\" Getting this label right protects the detection from being weakened.\n\n### The Asymmetric Cost of Errors\n\nThe two error types do not cost the same, and internalizing this shapes how you triage. The cost of a false positive is analyst time: minutes to hours, annoying but bounded and recoverable. The cost of a false negative, or of misclosing a true positive as a false positive, is an intrusion that runs unchecked: dwell time, lateral movement, exfiltration, ransomware.\n\nThat asymmetry is why the discipline is \"assume nothing, verify with data.\" It does not mean escalate everything, an analyst who escalates every alert is as useless as one who closes everything. It means that before you close an alert as benign, you should be able to point to the specific evidence that makes it benign, not just a vibe that it is probably fine. When you cannot find that evidence quickly, the correct move is to keep investigating or escalate, because the downside of being wrong is not symmetric.\n\n| Disposition | What It Means |\n| --- | --- |\n| True positive | Correctly identifies genuinely malicious or unauthorized activity |\n| False positive | Fired, but the underlying activity is not malicious |\n| True negative | The silent, correct non-alert, nothing bad happened and nothing fired |\n| False negative | Something bad happened and no alert fired at all |\n| Benign true positive | The rule correctly caught the behavior, but the activity is authorized and harmless |",
        "codeExample": "// Triage disposition decision aid (pseudocode, not executable)\n// Run these questions in order against every alert before you close it.\nif (activity_is_malicious_or_unauthorized) {\n  if (rule_intended_to_catch_this) disposition = \"TRUE POSITIVE -> investigate / escalate\";\n} else if (activity_is_real_but_authorized) {\n  // rule worked, behavior is sanctioned (pentest, admin task, scanner)\n  disposition = \"BENIGN TRUE POSITIVE -> close with justification + change ticket\";\n} else if (activity_did_not_actually_occur_as_described) {\n  disposition = \"FALSE POSITIVE -> close, consider tuning the rule\";\n}\n// Guardrail: you may only pick a benign disposition if you can cite the\n// specific evidence (ticket, baseline, owner confirmation) that makes it benign.\n// \"It's probably fine\" is not evidence. When in doubt, the asymmetric cost of a\n// missed true positive means: keep digging or escalate."
