      {
        "heading": "SOAR and Automation: When the Machine Does the First Ten Minutes",
        "content": "A SOC drowns not because its analysts are slow but because the *volume* of alerts and the *repetition* of the early investigative steps are enormous. For a huge share of alerts, the first ten minutes are the same mechanical work every time: look up the IP's reputation, check the user's recent activity, see if the hash is known, gather the surrounding events. **SOAR (Security Orchestration, Automation, and Response**) is the technology that hands that repetitive work to a machine, and understanding it explains where the SOC of today is heading.\n\nSOAR does two related things, captured in its name:\n\n- **Orchestration and enrichment (automating the gathering).** A SOAR **playbook** (an automated workflow triggered by an alert) can, in seconds, query threat intelligence for every indicator, pull the user's role and recent logins, check the endpoint's status in the EDR, and assemble it all into the case *before a human ever looks*. The analyst opens an alert that already has its context attached, instead of spending ten minutes collecting it.\n- **Automated response (automating the action).** For high-confidence, well-understood situations, a playbook can also *act*: isolate an endpoint, disable an account, block an IP at the firewall, or detonate a file in a sandbox. Either fully automatically for the clearest cases, or by presenting the analyst a one-click, pre-approved action.\n\nThe judgment SOAR demands is where the real lesson sits, and it is a balance you will hear debated in every SOC:\n\n- **Automate the gathering aggressively; automate the deciding cautiously.** Enrichment is almost always safe to automate, collecting context harms nothing. *Response* actions (isolating a server, disabling an executive's account) carry real business risk, so the safest designs keep a human in the loop for consequential decisions, reserving full automation for narrow, high-confidence, low-blast-radius cases.\n- **Automation amplifies whatever you point it at.** A good playbook makes the SOC dramatically faster; a bad one automates a mistake at machine speed. This is exactly why the detection-as-code discipline from the previous section matters. Automated actions should be built and reviewed with the same care as code.\n\nFor the analyst, SOAR changes the job rather than removing it: the tedious first-ten-minutes gathering is done for you, which frees your attention for the part machines are bad at. *judgment*, the \"so what?\" that decides whether the enriched, assembled case is actually an incident. The metric it moves is **MTTR** (mean time to respond): automate the mechanical minutes and the human gets to the decision far sooner."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Log Source Coverage: You Cannot Detect What You Do Not Collect",
        "content": "Every detection rule, every correlation, every clever query in this lesson rests on one silent precondition: **the relevant log has to be flowing into the SIEM in the first place.** This is the single most under-appreciated truth in security operations, and it is where many real failures actually originate, not in a missed detection, but in a source that was never collected at all.\n\nThe principle is blunt: **a SIEM can only see what it ingests.** If a system is not sending its logs, an un-onboarded VPN appliance, a SaaS application nobody connected, a new cloud account outside the collection scope, a network segment with no sensor, then it is a **blind spot**, and no rule, however brilliant, can fire on data that does not exist. You cannot query your way around a gap; you can only notice it. This is the \"garbage in, garbage out\" problem restated for detection: no coverage in means no detection out.\n\nBecause coverage decides what is even *possible* to detect, mature SOCs treat it as a first-class concern rather than an afterthought:\n\n- **Map coverage to the threats you care about.** A common practice is to lay your collected log sources against the **MITRE ATT&CK** techniques you most need to detect, producing a heat-map of where you are strong and where you are blind. MITRE's free ATT&CK Navigator is the usual tool: you load a layer file that colours each technique by coverage (the example below), and you can overlay a layer of a specific threat group's techniques to see which of its moves you would miss. That turns \"are we covered?\" from a vague worry into a specific, visible gap list, and directly drives what to onboard next.\n- **Onboarding is security work, not just IT plumbing.** Getting a critical source into the SIEM (and confirming it keeps flowing) is one of the highest-leverage things a SOC can do, because it converts an invisible blind spot into detectable ground.\n- **Silent source failure is its own risk.** A source that *was* collected but quietly stopped (a broken forwarder, a changed log format) recreates the blind spot without anyone deciding to. Monitoring that your sources are still reporting is part of keeping coverage honest.\n\nFor the analyst, this reframes a common frustration into a professional contribution. When an investigation stalls because the evidence was never collected, the correct output is not \"inconclusive\" but a **named coverage gap**: \"I could not determine initial access because we do not ingest logs from the VPN appliance.\" That single sentence is more valuable than a guess. It tells the organisation exactly where it is defenceless and turns a dead-end investigation into a concrete improvement. The through-line for the whole SIEM topic: a SIEM is only as good as what you feed it, and knowing what you *don't* collect is as important as knowing how to query what you do.",
        "codeExample": "// A minimal ATT&CK Navigator layer (JSON) mapping detection coverage\n// This is the actual file format the Navigator tool imports and renders as a heat-map.\n{\n  \"name\": \"SOC Detection Coverage - Credential Access\",\n  \"versions\": { \"attack\": \"18\", \"navigator\": \"5.1.0\", \"layer\": \"4.5\" },\n  \"domain\": \"enterprise-attack\",\n  \"techniques\": [\n    {\n      \"techniqueID\": \"T1110.003\",\n      \"comment\": \"Covered - password spray analytics rule on SigninLogs\",\n      \"color\": \"#4caf50\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"techniqueID\": \"T1110.001\",\n      \"comment\": \"Partial - raw telemetry present, no dedicated rule yet\",\n      \"color\": \"#ffc107\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"techniqueID\": \"T1558.003\",\n      \"comment\": \"No coverage - Kerberoasting: no DC log source onboarded\",\n      \"color\": \"#f44336\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"legendItems\": [\n    { \"label\": \"Covered\", \"color\": \"#4caf50\" },\n    { \"label\": \"Partial telemetry only\", \"color\": \"#ffc107\" },\n    { \"label\": \"No coverage\", \"color\": \"#f44336\" }\n  ]\n}\n// This single layer IS the coverage gap list: T1558.003 in red tells the SOC\n// exactly which technique and which missing log source to prioritize next."
      },
      {
        "heading": "UEBA: Detecting the Anomaly Nobody Wrote a Rule For",
        "content": "Every detection family covered earlier (signatures, thresholds, correlation, scheduled analytics) shares one trait: a human decided in advance what \"bad\" looks like and wrote a rule for it. **UEBA, User and Entity Behavior Analytics**, is a fifth, fundamentally different family: instead of matching a predefined pattern, it builds a statistical or machine-learning **baseline** of what *normal* looks like for each user or entity, and flags meaningful *deviation* from that baseline, even when no rule was ever written for the specific behavior observed.\n\n### How Baselining Works\n\nA UEBA engine watches an entity (a user, a host, a service account) over a training window (commonly 30 days) and learns its normal patterns: typical login hours, typical source countries, typical volume of file access, typical set of servers touched, typical peer group behavior (comparing a user against others with the same job role). Once a baseline exists, new activity is scored against it. A single anomaly rarely triggers anything on its own; UEBA's real value is **cumulative risk scoring**: an unusual login time contributes a small risk increment, an unfamiliar destination adds more, an unprecedented volume of downloads adds more still, and the entity's aggregate risk score rises until it crosses a threshold that surfaces as an alert. This is precisely why UEBA catches what rule-based detection misses: a \"low and slow\" insider or a patient attacker who never trips any single threshold can still accumulate an abnormal *pattern* over days or weeks.\n\n### What It Catches That Rules Do Not\n\nUEBA is particularly suited to threats that are ambiguous at the single-event level: an insider slowly exfiltrating data below any DLP threshold, a compromised account behaving in ways that are individually plausible but collectively out of character, or a service account that starts being used interactively. None of which cleanly matches a signature or a fixed threshold, because the \"bad\" is defined by *deviation from that specific entity's own history*, not a universal rule. Microsoft Sentinel's UEBA module, Splunk UBA, Exabeam, and Securonix all implement this pattern, typically surfacing entities with a computed risk score and the specific behavioral indicators that drove it, for example an IdentityInfo/BehaviorAnalytics record flagging \"ImpossibleTravelActivity\" or \"AnomalousSignInLocation\" alongside the numeric contribution each made to the total score.\n\n### The Trade-offs\n\nUEBA is not a free upgrade over rule-based detection, and a good analyst knows its limits. It needs a **cold-start baseline period**: a new user or a newly onboarded log source has no history yet, so anomaly scoring is unreliable until enough data accumulates. It can be **noisy in the opposite direction**: a role change, a new project, or simply an employee's vacation-return catch-up work can look anomalous and generate a flood of low-value alerts if not tuned. And UEBA outputs are often less **explainable** than a rule, \"risk score 87, driven by three behavioral indicators\" requires more enrichment work to turn into a defensible verdict than a rule whose logic you can read in one query. In practice, mature SOCs treat UEBA as a complement to rule-based detection, not a replacement: rules catch the known-bad fast and cheap, and UEBA is the safety net for the unknown-bad that no one thought to write a rule for.\n\n| Aspect | Rule-Based Detection (signatures/thresholds/correlation) | UEBA |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Basis | A human-defined pattern of known-bad behavior | A statistical baseline of the entity's own normal behavior |\n| Catches | Known techniques, volume spikes, defined multi-step chains | Low-and-slow, insider threats, plausible-but-abnormal patterns |\n| Output | Binary: rule matched or did not | A cumulative risk score plus contributing indicators |\n| Weakness | Blind to anything not explicitly defined | Cold-start baseline period; noisier; less directly explainable |",
        "codeExample": "// KQL: querying Microsoft Sentinel's UEBA-derived BehaviorAnalytics table\n// for entities whose risk score crossed a threshold in the last 24 hours\nBehaviorAnalytics\n| where TimeGenerated > ago(24h)\n| where InvestigationPriority >= 7          // Sentinel's 0-10 UEBA risk score\n| project TimeGenerated, UserName, ActivityType,\n          InvestigationPriority,\n          ActivityInsights                  // the specific indicators that drove the score\n| sort by InvestigationPriority desc\n\n// Example ActivityInsights payload for one high-risk entity (conceptual):\n// {\n//   \"UsualActivity\": false,\n//   \"FirstTimeUserConnectedFromCountry\": true,\n//   \"UsualForCountry\": false,\n//   \"CountryUncommonlyConnectedFromAmongPeers\": true\n// }\n// No single field here is a \"rule match\" -- it is the CONVERGENCE of several\n// low-level deviations from this user's own baseline that produced the risk score."
      }
